\begin_layout Plain Layout
 
\series bold
Economic KKT cookbook.
\series default
 For 
\begin_inset Formula $\max_{z\ge0}f(z)$
\end_inset

 subject to slacks 
\begin_inset Formula $g_j(z)\ge0$
\end_inset

, define 
\begin_inset Formula \[
D_i=f_i+\sum_j\lambda_j g_{j,i}.
\]
\end_inset

Then write 
\begin_inset Formula $0\le z_i\perp -D_i\ge0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $0\le\lambda_j\perp g_j(z)\ge0$
\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset Formula $a\perp b$
\end_inset

 means 
\begin_inset Formula $ab=0$
\end_inset

. Guess which choices are positive and which constraints bind, solve the resulting equalities, and verify every remaining sign. For a variable whose sign is already implied by other constraints, use 
\begin_inset Formula $D_i=0$
\end_inset

 and do not add a redundant multiplier. 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset VSpace 8pt
\end_inset

 
\series bold
Model.
\series default
 A household chooses parental consumption 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

, a divisible fertility index 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

, and total human-capital investment across children 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

. If 
\begin_inset Formula $n>0$
\end_inset

, quality per child is 
\begin_inset Formula $q=H/n$
\end_inset

. Preferences are 
\begin_inset Formula \[
U(c,n,H)=c+6n-\tfrac12 n^2+4H-\tfrac12H^2.
\]
\end_inset

The constraints are 
\begin_inset Formula \[
c+pn+kH+\tfrac12H^2\le y,\; c\ge1,\; n\ge0,\; sn\le H\le4n.
\]
\end_inset

Here 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is income, 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 is the direct resource cost of fertility, 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 is the linear price of total human-capital investment, and 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 is minimum quality per child. The cone 
\begin_inset Formula $sn\le H\le4n$
\end_inset

 means 
\begin_inset Formula $s\le q\le4$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $n>0$
\end_inset

 and forces 
\begin_inset Formula $H=0$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $n=0$
\end_inset

. Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $H\ge0$
\end_inset

 is already implied and needs no separate multiplier. The quadratic terms are a tractable static approximation; no prior knowledge of Galor is required.
\end_layout

